/* book Worked example: Q: A CMOS process uses these masks, one lithography step each: n-well, active, poly, n+ source/drain, p+ source/drain, contact, metal 1, via, metal 2, pad. (a) How many lithography steps? (b) A die is 0.5 cm² and the line has D = 1 defect/cm²; use Y = e−A·D for yield. (c) How many steps if metal 2 is dropped? (d) How many good dies does a 300 mm wafer give? Use N ≈ π(d/2)²/A − πd/√(2A).
   1. Count the masks: n-well, active, poly, n+ S/D, p+ S/D, contact, metal 1, via, metal 2, pad. That is 10 masks, and one lithography step each gives 10 steps.
   2. For yield, A = 0.5 cm² and D = 1 defect/cm². A·D = 0.5 × 1 = 0.5. Y = e−0.5 = 0.6065. As a percentage, 60.7%, about 61%.
   3. Drop metal 2. The via connects metal 1 to metal 2, so the via mask also goes. Steps = 10 − 2 = 8.
   4. (d) d = 30 cm: π × 15²/0.5 = 1413.7 and π × 30/√(2 × 0.5) = 94.2, so N = 1413.7 − 94.2 = 1319 whole dies.
   5. Good dies = N × Y = 1319 × 0.6065 = 800.
   Answer: (a) 10 lithography steps; (b) Y = 0.607, about 61%; (c) 8 steps; (d) 1319 dies per wafer, of which about 800 work. */
