/* book: lead: Every CMOS chip needs both an NMOS and a PMOS. An NMOS needs a p-type body; a PMOS needs an n-type body. One silicon substrate can supply only one doping, so the other device must sit in a locally doped well. The p-well, n-well and twin-tub processes are three answers to the same question: which device goes inside a well, and how freely can each body be doped? The choice fixes mask count, body res */
