/* book Worked example: Q: A twin-tub process lets the designer choose the NMOS body doping. Compare an NMOS built straight in a lightly doped p-substrate (NA = 1×10¹⁵ cm⁻³) with one built in a p-well (NA = 1×10¹⁷ cm⁻³). Both have tox = 10 nm, so Cox = 3.45×10⁻⁷ F/cm². Take εSi = 1.04×10⁻¹² F/cm, q = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C, ni = 1×10¹⁰ cm⁻³ and kT/q = 0.0259 V. (a) Find the body-effect coefficient γ = √(2qεSiNA)/Cox for each. (b) Find the threshold rise ΔVT = γ(√(2φF + VSB) − √(2φF)) at VSB = 1 V, with φF = (kT/q)·ln(NA/ni).
   1. Substrate, NA = 1×10¹⁵: 2qεSiNA = 2 × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 1.04×10⁻¹² × 10¹⁵ = 3.31×10⁻¹⁶; the square root is 1.82×10⁻⁸.
   2. γ = 1.82×10⁻⁸ / 3.45×10⁻⁷ = 0.053 V½.
   3. p-well, NA = 1×10¹⁷: the doping is 100 times larger, so γ is √100 = 10 times larger: γ = 0.53 V½.
   4. φF: substrate 0.0259 × ln(10⁵) = 0.298 V, so 2φF = 0.596 V; p-well 0.0259 × ln(10⁷) = 0.417 V, so 2φF = 0.835 V.
   5. Substrate: ΔVT = 0.053 × (√1.596 − √0.596) = 0.053 × (1.263 − 0.772) = 0.026 V.
   6. p-well: ΔVT = 0.53 × (√1.835 − √0.835) = 0.53 × (1.355 − 0.914) = 0.23 V.
   Answer: γ = 0.053 V½ in the substrate and 0.53 V½ in the p-well; at VSB = 1 V the threshold rises by 0.026 V and 0.23 V. The heavier well gives about nine times the body effect, which is why a twin-tub process sets each well dose on purpose. */
