/* book exam traps: Saying the n-well process puts the NMOS in a well. It does not: the NMOS stays in the p-substrate and only the PMOS is in the n-well. | Swapping the bodies: in a p-well process the NMOS is in the p-well and the PMOS is in the n-substrate. | Thinking twin-tub is just two names for one well. It uses two independently doped wells and one extra well mask, so both Vt and body effect can be set. | Using the mobility ratio upside down. Holes are slower, so Wp/Wn = μn/μp is greater than one. | Believing the well is a thin surface layer. It is a deep region whose doping and depth affect parasitic capacitance and latch-up. */
